#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134689} :activity/id #uuid "542ba334-8a63-5347-814a-76bcfa5bc249" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049864 :topic/id #uuid "1973989c-ebc8-5a88-b58e-c9b9de5f86ee" :topic/math-academy-id 2128} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135056 :tutorial/content "Consider the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe already know how to find an orthogonal basis for $2$-dimensional subspaces. But, how can we do it for subspaces with higher dimensions, like $V?$\n\nGood news is that we can extend the **Gram-Schmidt process**. Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, let $S=\\textrm{Span}(\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2).$ We find ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5b/5b52c26738f2b4dd577ab4148ef7673ad4a1aa53c82a6a6c1e7bf46c0ff36df0.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}),$ $\\mathbf{a}_2(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}),$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ This means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\perp S.$ Moreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-(\\frac{2}{2})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{3}{3})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "066a02aa-4c3e-5e75-8147-7e723bac3b9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b8d3614-1e0d-5f26-94a2-81b1f24e5df1" :step/math-academy-id 20262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134690}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133605 :question/id #uuid "683556db-c65d-5279-ac14-7dfaa496db0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5584"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f585bd05-7dac-55b1-a0ab-f8320242a5df" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360630}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis of a Span Given Some Orthogonal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "9ca65108-e04c-5dd4-9c81-fa10af333e97" :step/math-academy-id 20263 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134797}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135057 :tutorial/content "But what do we do if the original basis we are given doesn't contain any orthogonal vectors? Well, then we make them orthogonal using the Gram-Schmidt process!\n\nGiven a basis $\\,\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\ldots,\\mathbf{a}_n\\}$ for a non zero subspace $V$, we can get an orthogonal basis for $V$ by defining the vectors\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\ ⋮\\,\\, & =\\,⋮ \\\\ {v}_{n} & ={a}_{n}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{n}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{n}-⋯-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{n-1}}\\,{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe set of vectors $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n\\}$ will be an orthogonal basis for $V$.\n\nHere, we build up the orthogonal basis one vector at a time, by taking away from each basis vector its orthogonal projection onto each of the $1$-dimensional subspaces spanned by one of the orthogonal basis vectors found already.\n\nFurthermore, if we want to find an *orthonormal* basis, we just have to normalize the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_i$ for $i=1,2,\\dots,n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3f649a6-0268-539c-a952-a33eeaa25744" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3634 :tutorial/title "Gram-Schmidt Process"} :step/id #uuid "3d2dec80-add8-5035-b3b2-de9867a75aad" :step/math-academy-id 20264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133606 :question/id #uuid "87d88110-e10d-553c-983d-6c22d3f19895" :question/math-academy-id "e-5585"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9870ad37-3e54-5403-936c-09a321d4a0cf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366697}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis Using the Gram-Schmidt Process"} :step/id #uuid "d25a0643-79f7-54c9-aee4-b85ff777d4ae" :step/math-academy-id 20265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133861 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133677 :question/id #uuid "06f90a75-b288-5975-9e74-994c180be4d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "866ab12b-7f51-55f8-9e7b-e39f9c3b4b44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392415}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix Given Some Orthogonal Columns"} :step/id #uuid "b6a4c4a6-8981-577f-95e3-60a561156de9" :step/math-academy-id 31080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134691}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process in the General Case" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134007}]}
